# Stage 6 — classes and element ids in rendered markup, and the stylesheets
# that select them.
#
# This is the visible one. gform_wrapper alone is 3702 occurrences and is the
# id and class on every form on the site, so any custom CSS or Elementor
# styling written against .gform_wrapper stops applying. Taken deliberately:
# this build is for new sites, and an old site is fixed by changing the
# selector.
#
# Both sides always move together — the class attribute and the selector, the
# id attribute and the getElementById, the concatenated prefix and the JS that
# strips it back off ('gform_wrapper_' . $id in form_display.php and the
# .replace( 'gform_wrapper_', '' ) in the Elementor loader and datepicker.js).
#
# Also included here are the concatenation prefixes whose runtime result is
# markup: the wrapper, page, progressbar, step, card icon, note and name-field
# classes, and the ids for the submit, previous, next, save, resume, ajax
# frame and multifile upload elements. gf_feed_is_active_ comes with them, and
# it is a live fault rather than a cosmetic one: PHP registers
# wp_ajax_kdna_feed_is_active_{slug} while form_admin.js posts
# action: "gf_feed_is_active_" + slug, so toggling a feed active or inactive in
# any add-on has been doing nothing. The gf_ -> kdna_ rule merges the two.
#
# Held back: gf_dimissed_, which builds an option key stored in the database.

